      "paperId": "bc17e0a6705b6b92e2fb265f0a915be339e026fd",
      "title": "Herder\u2019s Nondualism",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Vicki A. Spencer",
          "authorId": "2372895680"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "ABSTRACT Herder\u2019s nondualism is one of the most misrepresented and underappreciated aspects of his thought outside of Herder scholarship that has often resulted in its dismissal as fundamentally contradictory. Yet many of the problems associated with Herder\u2019s thought dissipate by appreciating the pivotal role of his nondualism in his philosophical approach. This article shows that for Herder, the mind and body, reason and emotion, literal and figurative language, and nature and culture do not exist in a hierarchical relationship in which the mind, reason, literal language, and culture are deemed to have greater validity than their apparent opposites, but are mutually dependent in an interactive relationship that blurs the boundaries of these categories. Herder\u2019s nondualism therefore preempts recent critiques of dichotomous thinking that are often associated with Cartesian rationalism and the Enlightenment. It also differs from Leibniz\u2019s anti-dualist position which challenges the idea that reality is composed of fundamentally distinct entities or substances, like the mind and the body, but denies interaction between them. It does not follow that Herder rejects Enlightenment concerns, although, as I will argue, he does offer an alternative approach to the dualist, anti-dualist, and dichotomous positions in Enlightenment philosophy.",
